I also think the interface plays a role.  

As I look in the source at possible chest traps, I see they can be
pretty nasty.  However only 1 in 10 chests is trapped; for a 
character with high Luck, most traps will fizzle; and for a character
with high Luck, the overwhelming majority of traps are weak traps.  So 
it seems for instance that for a character with maximum Luck, only
1 in 3400 chests will exhibit the worst result of exploding, taking 6d6 
hitpoints, waking monsters, and destroying all objects at that location.
(Although worse results might be insta-death by poisoning, or object 
destruction by fire and electricity.)

I've certainly never seen a chest explode in all my hacking.
So it may be that---with it taking so many keystrokes---it's just not 
worth the bother to check for traps, once you have high Luck.

